  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mrs. ___. I'm Dr. ___, one of your hospital doctors. You came in with nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. Can you tell me more about how you've been feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it started suddenly last night. I’ve had stomach pain, and I’ve thrown up a few times. The diarrhea is watery and greenish. I haven’t had a fever, but I feel weak."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you mentioned you might have vomited some of your pills this morning?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I think so. I couldn’t keep them down. I take a lot of medications—morphine, blood pressure pills, inhalers…"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll make sure you get back on track with those. You also said you’ve been more short of breath and coughing lately?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it’s been getting worse over the past week. I’m on antibiotics at home—cefpodoxime—and I’ve been using my inhalers more."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We did a chest X-ray today, and it shows a new shadow in your right upper lung. This is concerning for pneumonia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Pneumonia? Again? I’ve had that before. Is it serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s something we need to treat, but we’ve started you on an antibiotic called levofloxacin. That should help clear it up over the next few days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thank you. What about the diarrhea? That’s what brought me in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We tested your stool, and it’s not a C. difficile infection or any other common bacteria. That’s good news. But we did a CT scan, and it showed something else."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What did it show?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your GJ tube—the feeding tube that goes into your stomach and small intestine—isn’t in the right place. The part that should be in your jejunum has pulled back into your stomach. That could be causing the diarrhea and nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh no. That happened before, didn’t it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and we’ll need to fix it. Interventional radiology will replace the tube tomorrow. In the meantime, we’re holding your tube feeds to let your gut rest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will they put me to sleep?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll get some sedation—fentanyl and Versed—to keep you comfortable. It’s a common procedure, and they’ll make sure the new tube is in the right spot with X-ray guidance."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I trust you. What about my pain? I’ve been on morphine, and it helps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2174-07-03 17:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep you on your regular morphine dose. We know you have chronic pain from arthritis and fibromyalgia, so we’ll manage that carefully while you’re here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2174-07-04 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your stool test came back negative for infections, which confirms our suspicion that the tube feeds were likely causing the diarrhea due to the speed or positioning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2174-07-04 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So once the tube is fixed, I can start feeding again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2174-07-04 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, but we’ll start slowly. Nutrition will help us plan the best way—either giving the feedings more slowly or adjusting the rate to prevent diarrhea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2174-07-04 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been thinking… I’d like to try to stop using the tube altogether. Can I?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2174-07-04 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We understand. But right now, with your esophageal stricture and history of swallowing issues, it’s safer to keep it. You did well with calories by mouth on two of the three days we checked, so you can eat when you can, and use the tube when you need to."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2174-07-04 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. I just hate being dependent on it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2174-07-05 11:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The radiology team is about to replace your GJ tube. They’ll make sure it’s in the right place in your jejunum. Any questions before we start?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2174-07-05 11:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I think I understand. Will it hurt?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2174-07-05 11:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll be sedated, so you’ll feel very relaxed and won’t feel pain. Afterward, you might have some mild discomfort, but we’ll manage that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2174-07-05 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The procedure went well. Your new MIC GJ tube is in place, with the tip in the jejunum. We’ll wait a few hours and then start a slow refeed to see how you tolerate it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2174-07-05 13:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. I hope this stops the diarrhea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2174-07-06 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You had some nausea and vomiting overnight. We did an abdominal X-ray, and there’s no blockage or free air, which is reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2174-07-06 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I felt better by this morning. My daughter says I get nauseated often at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2174-07-06 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s something we’ve noted. We’ll keep monitoring. The tube feeds are running slowly, and we’ll adjust as needed. Your lungs are clearer today—less wheezing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2174-07-06 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been using my inhalers regularly. Is the pneumonia getting better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2174-07-06 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your cough is still there, but it may be partly from bronchitis. We lowered your prednisone back to 10 mg since your breathing improved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2174-07-07 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your diarrhea has improved since stopping and restarting the feeds slowly. We think the speed was contributing to it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2174-07-07 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I need to go slow with the feeds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2174-07-07 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Nutrition recommends either giving one can of Isosource 1.5 three times a day slowly, or running it at 75 mL per hour for 10 hours. You can eat by mouth too—just supplement when needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2174-07-07 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I can do that. What about my follow-up for the esophageal stricture?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2174-07-07 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "GI is aware. You wanted to delay the endoscopy, and they’ll reschedule it. We’ll make sure you have an appointment before discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2174-07-08 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. Your pneumonia is resolving, the tube is working, and your blood pressure is stable on your usual meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2174-07-08 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel much better. When can I go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2174-07-08 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning for discharge tomorrow. You’ll go home with services—someone to help you at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2174-07-08 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. Will I keep taking the antibiotic?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2174-07-08 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, just two more days of levofloxacin. Then you’ll finish the course at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2174-07-09 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Final check before discharge. Your lungs are clear, your abdomen is tender but not concerning, and you’re walking with your walker—great progress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2174-07-09 11:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m ready to go home. Thank you for fixing the tube and helping me feel better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2174-07-09 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Remember: take your tube feeds slowly, use your inhalers, and call if you have fever, trouble breathing, or severe nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2174-07-09 11:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I have follow-up with GI and my regular doctors?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2174-07-09 11:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, all scheduled. You’ve done really well. We’ll see you in clinic soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2174-07-03 17:18:00",
    "admission_end": "2174-07-10 13:50:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "Mrs. ___ was admitted for acute nausea, vomiting, and profuse green watery diarrhea, with a concurrent history of worsening shortness of breath and cough. Imaging revealed a new right upper lobe infiltrate consistent with pneumonia, treated with intravenous levofloxacin. A CT scan showed malposition of her GJ tube, with the jejunal limb pulled back into the stomach, contributing to her gastrointestinal symptoms. The tube was successfully replaced via interventional radiology. Diarrhea resolved after holding and then restarting tube feeds at a slower rate. She improved clinically, with resolution of respiratory symptoms and stable chronic pain management. She was discharged home with services on a 10-day course of levofloxacin, with plans for outpatient GI follow-up for esophageal stricture and continued management of chronic conditions including asthma, hypertension, and rheumatoid arthritis.",
    "problems": [
      "Pneumonia, organism unspecified",
      "Chronic obstructive asthma with (acute) exacerbation",
      "Chronic diastolic heart failure",
      "Mechanical complication of gastrostomy",
      "Diverticulosis of colon (without mention of hemorrhage)",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Chronic pain syndrome",
      "Stricture and stenosis of esophagus",
      "Rheumatoid arthritis",
      "Anemia, unspecified",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Nausea with vomiting",
      "Diarrhea",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of breast",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of bronchus and lung",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of trachea"
    ]
  }
}
